Q: How can I make a recursive function return an array of a given number set to zero? A: <p>Let's take a look at your code:</p>
<pre><code>function rangeUp( n, numberList = [] ) {
    if( n &lt; 0 ) return console.error( &quot;The number is invalid.&quot; );
    if( n == 0 ) return numberList.push( n );
    return rangeUp(--n, numberList);
}
</code></pre>
<p>The source of the error is in the line:</p>
<pre><code>if( n == 0 ) return numberList.push( n );
</code></pre>
<p>specificaly <strong>numberList.push( n )</strong>, only appears in that line, so the general behavior defined by it is that only when <strong>n = 0</strong> will it add <strong>n</strong> to <strong>numberList</strong>, and at that point it returns.</p>
<p>The solution is simple: separate the value load from the return.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function rangeUp( n, numberList = [] ) {
    if( n &lt; 0 ) return console.error( "The number is invalid." );

      // in each iteration, we add the value of **n**.
    numberList.push( n );

      // if that's the case, we'll return the list.
    if( n == 0 ) return numberList;

      // we perform the recursion
    return rangeUp( --n, numberList );
}

console.log( rangeUp( 5 ) ) </code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>
